 "title": "Never Prepare for Conversations to Sound Better",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator claims that preparing for conversations beforehand results in giving rehearsed answers instead of responding naturally. To become a better conversationalist, one should speak to more people without prior preparation.",
 "key_points": [
  "Never prepare for conversations beforehand.",
  "Conversations are between two people, and you can never fully predict what the other person will say.",
  "Preparing beforehand leads to giving rehearsed answers instead of responding to the other person's statements.",
  "A prepared conversation is never well-rounded and ends up being just a prepared presentation.",
  "Improve your conversation skills by speaking to more people and not preparing beforehand."
 ],
 "content": "How to become a disgustingly good conversationalist? Never prepare. I said it. A conversation is between two people and you can never fully, completely, in all its nuances predict what the other person is going to say. But when you do prepare beforehand, what ends up happening is that instead of responding to their statements, you start giving out rehearsed answers. As a result, this conversation is never rounded, at best it's a prepared presentation. But then how do we get better? By speaking to more people and by not preparing it beforehand.",
 "status": "done",
